    {
      "parameters": {
        "jsCode": "const documents = $input.all();\nconst ticket = $('Code in JavaScript').first().json;\n\n// Chroma returns a DISTANCE: lower = better match\nconst THRESHOLD = 0.75;\n\nconst scores = documents\n  .map(i => i.json.score)\n  .filter(s => typeof s === 'number');\n\nconst best_score = scores.length ? Math.min(...scores) : null;\nconst kb_match_weak = best_score === null || best_score > THRESHOLD;\n\nconst knowledge = kb_match_weak\n  ? 'NO RELEVANT KNOWLEDGE BASE ARTICLES WERE FOUND FOR THIS TICKET.'\n  : documents.map((item, index) => {\n      const doc = item.json.document;\n      return `Document ${index + 1}\\n${doc?.pageContent ?? ''}`;\n    }).join('\\n\\n---\\n\\n');\n\nreturn [{\n  json: {\n    ticket_id: ticket.ticket_id,\n    category: ticket.category,\n    priority: ticket.priority,\n    needs_human: ticket.needs_human,\n    should_escalate: ticket.should_escalate,\n    reasoning: ticket.reasoning,\n    message: ticket.message,\n    knowledge,\n    best_score,\n    kb_match_weak\n  }\n}];"
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.code",
      "typeVersion": 2,
      "position": [-160, 48],
      "id": "19b1a9d1-1092-432e-ba58-55fc27ee3cb5",
      "name": "Code in JavaScript2"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "promptType": "define",
        "text": "=You are the customer support assistant for Brightboard.\n\nDraft a helpful, professional reply to the customer.\n\nCUSTOMER TICKET:\nTicket ID: {{ $json.ticket_id }}\nCategory: {{ $json.category }}\nPriority: {{ $json.priority }}\nMessage: {{ $json.message }}\n\nKNOWLEDGE BASE:\n{{ $json.knowledge }}\n\nRULES:\n1. Use only the knowledge base for company policies and technical instructions. Never invent policies, features, prices, deadlines, or exceptions.\n2. If the knowledge base does not cover part of the issue, say it needs further investigation. Where useful, ask for specific details, such as device, operating system, app version, screenshots, or steps to reproduce.\n3. Address every concern the customer raised.\n4. Be polite, concise, and empathetic. Write plain text with no markdown formatting.\n5. For refund, billing, charge, or cancellation requests, explain the relevant policy from the knowledge base. Never say a refund or credit has been approved, processed, or denied, and never reject a request for an exception. If the request appears to fall outside the standard policy, say so, and explain that the final decision rests with the support team. Only mention dates or time periods the customer actually stated.\n6. Never claim that an action has been taken (refunded, escalated, forwarded, flagged, assigned, or changed on the account). Do not promise follow-up times unless the knowledge base states them.\n7. Never mention internal details such as the category, priority, or how the ticket was classified.\n8. Never ask the customer for a password or full payment card details.\n9. Return only the customer-facing reply: no subject line, no notes, no explanations.\n10. Absence of information is not evidence. If the knowledge base does not mention a feature, setting, or policy, never say it does not exist, is not supported, or is not available. Instead say you could not confirm it from the available information and that the support team will check.\n11. Never write \"I'll pass this along\", \"I'll forward\", \"I'll let the team know\", or similar. Use wording like \"our team will review this\" only if it makes no promise of a specific action.",
        "batching": {}
      },
